.TH wall "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
wall \- send a message to all logged in users
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB/etc/wall\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
wall -- send a message to all logged in users
Usage:	/etc/wall
Should be invoked only by superuser.
.HE
.B wall
reads a message from the standard input, up to end of file, and writes
it, preceded by the banner
.B "Broadcast message ....."
and an audible bell, to the terminal of every user currently logged in,
as recorded in
.BR /etc/utmp .
It does not write to its own controlling terminal.
.PP
.B wall
takes no arguments and recognizes no options.
The message, including the banner, may be no longer than 512 bytes;
if the standard input exceeds this, wall stops reading, reports an
error, and exits without sending anything.
.PP
For each logged-in terminal that
.B wall
cannot open for writing (for example, because its owner has denied
messages with
.BR mesg ),
.B wall
prints a diagnostic naming the user and terminal and continues with the
next entry in
.BR /etc/utmp .
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 16n
\fB/etc/utmp\fR	list of logged-in users
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR write ,
.BR who ,
.BR msg ,
.B mesg
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B wall
prints
.B "Cannot open utmp."
and exits if
.B /etc/utmp
cannot be opened;
.B "Message too long."
if the input, together with the banner, exceeds 512 bytes; and, for
each terminal it cannot write to,
.B "Cannot send to name on tty."
before going on to the next user.
.SH NOTES
.B wall
should be invoked only by the superuser, per its
.B /etc/helpfile
entry; the source itself does not check who invokes it, so any user
who can execute
.B /etc/wall
may broadcast to every open terminal.
